window.docContents[44].resolve({"/Subgraphs/#section-building-partial-colourings":{"contents":"Fundamentally we are interested in partial colourings as a tool to build total\ncolourings, so we want to be able to turn a G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ into a\nG.Coloring γ.\n\n/-- A G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ is a G.Coloring γ -/\ndef ColoringOn.toColoring (C : G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ) : G.Coloring γ :=\n    ⟨C, fun h ↦ C.valid (by simpa using h)⟩\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.coe_toColoring (C : G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ) :\n    ⇑C.toColoring = ⇑C := rfl\n\n\n/-- A colouring gives rise to a partial colouring on any set -/\ndef Coloring.toColoringOn (C : G.Coloring γ) (s : Set V) : G.ColoringOn γ s :=\n    ⟨C, fun h ↦ C.valid (by simpa using h.2.2)⟩\n\n\nA graph is n-colourable if and only if G.ColorableOn n Set.univ.\n\nvariable (G)\n\n\nlemma colorableOn_univ_iff_colorable (n : ℕ) :\n    G.ColorableOn n (Set.univ : Set V) ↔ G.Colorable n := by\n  constructor <;> intro ⟨c⟩\n  · exact ⟨c.toColoring⟩\n  · exact ⟨c.toColoringOn _⟩\n\n\nThere are a few fundamental partial colourings that we will need to construct: the\npartial colouring of the empty set, the partial colouring of a singleton, and the\npartial colouring of a pair of non-adjacent vertices.\n\n/-- The partial colouring of the empty set by a colour c. -/\ndef coloringOnEmpty (c : γ) : G.ColoringOn γ ∅ :=\n  Coloring.mk (fun _ => c) fun h => h.1.elim\n\n\n/-- The partial colouring of the singleton set {a} by a colour c. -/\ndef coloringOnOfSingleton {γ : Type*} (a : V) (c : γ) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ ({a} : Set V) where\n  toFun := fun _ ↦ c\n  map_rel' := by simp\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma coloringOnOfSingleton_apply {γ : Type*} {a v : V} {c : γ} :\n  G.coloringOnOfSingleton a c v = c := rfl\n\n\n/-- The partial colouring of a pair of non-adjacent vertices by a colour c. -/\ndef coloringOnOfNotAdj {γ : Type*} {a b : V} (h : ¬ G.Adj a b) (c : γ) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ ({a, b} : Set V) where\n  toFun := fun _ ↦ c\n  map_rel' := by\n    rintro x y ⟨hx, hy, hxy⟩ -\n    aesop (add simp adj_comm)\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma coloringOnOfNotAdj_apply {γ : Type*} {a b v : V} {h : ¬ G.Adj a b} {c : γ} :\n  G.coloringOnOfNotAdj h c v = c := rfl\n\n\nvariable {G}\n\n\nWe can combine two partial colourings by extending the first using the second,\nprovided they are compatible in the sense that there is no edge between a vertex\ncoloured by C₁ and a vertex coloured only by C₂ using the same colour. This gives\na partial colouring on the union of the two sets.\n\n/-- We can combine colourings C₁ of s and C₂ of t if they are compatible i.e.\n∀ v w, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w to get a colouring of s ∪ t.\nIt agrees with C₁ on s and with C₂ on t \\ s. -/\ndef ColoringOn.union [DecidablePred (· ∈ s)] (C₁ : G.ColoringOn γ s)\n    (C₂ : G.ColoringOn γ t)\n    (h : ∀ ⦃v w⦄, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ (s ∪ t) where\n  toFun := fun v ↦ ite (v ∈ s) (C₁ v) (C₂ v)\n  map_rel' := by\n    rintro v w ⟨hv', hw', hadj⟩ hf\n    split_ifs at hf with hv hw hw\n    · exact C₁.valid ⟨hv, hw, hadj⟩ hf\n    · exact h hv ⟨hw'.resolve_left hw, hw⟩ hadj hf\n    · exact h hw ⟨hv'.resolve_left hv, hv⟩ hadj.symm hf.symm\n    · exact C₂.valid ⟨hv'.resolve_left hv, hw'.resolve_left hw, hadj⟩ hf\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.union_apply [DecidablePred (· ∈ s)] {v : V} (C₁ : G.ColoringOn γ s)\n    (C₂ : G.ColoringOn γ t)\n    (h : ∀ ⦃v w⦄, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w) :\n  (C₁.union C₂ h) v = ite (v ∈ s) (C₁ v) (C₂ v) := rfl\n\n\nThe extension of a colouring of s to insert a s using a colour c that is not\nused by C to colour a neighbour of a in s.\n\nprotected def ColoringOn.insert [DecidableEq V] (a : V) {c : γ}\n    (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : ∀ ⦃v⦄, v ∈ s → G.Adj a v → C v ≠ c) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ (insert a s) :=\n  ((G.coloringOnOfSingleton a c).union C (by\n    simp only [Set.mem_singleton_iff, Set.mem_sdiff, and_imp]\n    rintro _ _ rfl h' h1 had h2\n    exact h h' had h2.symm)).copy (by simp [Set.union_comm])\n\n\nSo C.insert a h colours a with c and agrees with C elsewhere.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.insert_apply [DecidableEq V] {a v : V} {c : γ}\n    (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ s → G.Adj a x → C x ≠ c) :\n    (C.insert a h) v = ite (v = a) c (C v) := by\n  rw [ColoringOn.insert, copy_apply, union_apply]\n  simp\n\n\nFinally we can relabel the colours of a partial colouring along any injective map (in\nparticular along any permutation of the colours): validity is preserved, since an\ninjection cannot merge two distinct colours.\n\n/-- Relabel the colours of a partial colouring along an embedding. -/\ndef ColoringOn.map {β : Type*} (f : γ ↪ β) (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) :\n    G.ColoringOn β s :=\n  (Embedding.completeGraph f).toHom.comp C\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma ColoringOn.map_apply {β : Type*} (f : γ ↪ β) (C : G.ColoringOn γ s)\n    {v : V} : C.map f v = f (C v) := rfl\n\n\nend partcol\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Partial Colourings*","header":"9.6.2. Building Partial Colourings","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-building-partial-colourings"},"/Theorems/#section-mantel-s-theorem":{"contents":"The case r = 2 of Turán's theorem is Mantel's theorem: a triangle-free graph on\nn vertices has at most ⌊n² / 4⌋ edges. Equality holds for the balanced complete\nbipartite graph turanGraph n 2.\n\n#check turanNumber_two -- turanNumber n 2 = n ^ 2 / 4\n\n\nFor example, the odd cycle C[7] is triangle-free, so it has at most ⌊49 / 4⌋ = 12\nedges. (decide checks that C[7] is triangle-free using the Decidable instance\nfor CliqueFree from The Finset Of N Cliques.)\n\nexample : #C[7].edgeFinset ≤ 7 ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  rw [← turanNumber_two]\n  exact CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le (by decide)\n\n\nBy Triangle Free Mycielskians, the Mycielskian of a\ntriangle-free graph on n vertices is a triangle-free graph on 2n + 1 vertices, so\nMantel's theorem bounds its number of edges by ⌊(2n + 1)² / 4⌋. For the Grötzsch\ngraph mμ[C[5]] this bound is 30, while the graph has 20 edges.\n\n#eval #mμ[C[5]].edgeFinset -- 20\n\n\nThe same applies to every iterated Mycielskian mμ^n C[5]: its clique number is\nalways 2 (Clique Number Of The Mycielskian), so Mantel's theorem\nbounds its number of edges, even though its chromatic number n + 3 grows without\nbound (Colouring Mycielskians).\n\nBipartite graphs are triangle-free, so they also have at most ⌊n² / 4⌋ edges.\nMathlib states this for all bipartite graphs, using ℕ∞-valued cardinalities so\nthat no finiteness assumption is needed.\n\n#check IsBipartite.four_mul_encard_edgeSet_le\n-- G.IsBipartite → 4 * G.edgeSet.encard ≤ ENat.card V ^ 2\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Extremal Graph Theory","header":"13.2.3. Mantel's Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-mantel-s-theorem"},"/Walks/#section-the-bondy-chv-tal-theorem":{"contents":"The key observation of Bondy and Chvátal is that the degree condition above is only\never needed one pair at a time: if u and v are distinct non-adjacent vertices\nwith deg u + deg v ≥ |V|, then G is Hamiltonian iff G with the edge u — v\nadded is Hamiltonian. Repeatedly adding such edges yields the closure of G.\n\nWe start with an easy lemma: reversal preserves being a Hamiltonian walk.\n(Reversal also preserves being a Hamiltonian cycle: see the exercises.)\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.isHamiltonian_reverse [DecidableEq V] {u v : V} {p : G.Walk u v} :\n    p.reverse.IsHamiltonian ↔ p.IsHamiltonian := by\n  simp only [Walk.IsHamiltonian, support_reverse, List.count_reverse]\n\n\nThe first step towards the Bondy–Chvátal theorem is a piece of cycle surgery: given a\nHamiltonian cycle c through an edge u — v, deleting that edge from c should\nleave a Hamiltonian path from u to v. Hamiltonicity plays no part in the surgery\nitself, so we cut open an arbitrary cycle and record what the resulting path\ninherits from it.\n\nWe first need to prove a simple lemma about darts of a cycle.\n\n#check darts_getElem_eq_getVert\n\n\nIf d is a dart of a cycle c : G.Walk u u and the first vertex of d is u, then\nthe second vertex of d is the second vertex of the cycle.\n\nlemma Walk.IsCycle.snd_eq_of_mem_darts {u : V} {c : G.Walk u u}\n    (hc : c.IsCycle) {d : G.Dart} (hd : d ∈ c.darts) (hfst : d.fst = u) :\n    d.snd = c.snd := by\n  obtain ⟨i, hi, rfl⟩ := List.mem_iff_getElem.1 hd\n  rw [darts_getElem_eq_getVert] at hfst ⊢\n  have hilt : i < c.length := c.length_darts ▸ hi\n  -- the dart leaves u, which only occurs at the ends, so its index is 0\n  have h0 : i = 0 := by\n    rcases (hc.getVert_endpoint_iff hilt.le).1 hfst with h | h <;> grind\n  subst h0\n  rfl\n\n\nDeleting an edge u — v from a cycle c leaves a path from u to v that is one\nedge shorter than c, visits exactly the vertices of c, and avoids u — v.\n\nThe edge u — v of the cycle is the image of a dart, which either points from u\nto v or from v to u (dart_edge_eq_mk'_iff'). Rotating the cycle to be based\nat the dart's first vertex makes the dart the first dart of the cycle, and the\nreversed tail of the rotated cycle then runs from the dart's first vertex to its\nsecond. It is a path, since the tail of a cycle is a path; it visits every vertex of\nthe cycle, since the base vertex reappears at the end of the tail; and it avoids the\ndeleted first edge, since a cycle is a trail, so no edge is repeated. This is the\nrequired path in the first case, and its reverse is the required path in the second.\n\nlemma Walk.IsCycle.exists_isPath_of_mem_edges {x u v : V} {c : G.Walk x x}\n    (hc : c.IsCycle) (he : s(u, v) ∈ c.edges) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.IsPath ∧ p.length + 1 = c.length ∧\n      (∀ w, w ∈ p.support ↔ w ∈ c.support) ∧ s(u, v) ∉ p.edges := by\n  classical\n  -- cutting c open at a dart from a to b gives an a-b path\n  have key : ∀ {a b : V}, ∀ d ∈ c.darts, d.fst = a → d.snd = b →\n      ∃ p : G.Walk a b, p.IsPath ∧ p.length + 1 = c.length ∧\n        (∀ w, w ∈ p.support ↔ w ∈ c.support) ∧ s(a, b) ∉ p.edges := by\n    rintro a b d hd rfl rfl\n    -- rotate c to be based at d.fst, so that d becomes its first dart\n    have ha := c.dart_fst_mem_support_of_mem_darts hd\n    have hr := hc.rotate ha\n    rw [hr.snd_eq_of_mem_darts ((c.rotate_darts _ ha).perm.mem_iff.2 hd) rfl]\n    -- the reversed tail of the rotated cycle is the required path\n    refine ⟨_, hr.isPath_tail.reverse, ?_, fun w => ?_, ?_⟩\n    · simpa using length_tail_add_one hr.not_nil\n    · -- d.fst is dropped from the start of the support but is still the end\n      have := cons_support_tail hr.not_nil\n      grind [support_reverse, end_mem_support, mem_support_rotate_iff]\n    · -- no edge is repeated, so the first edge does not occur in the tail\n      have hnd := hr.edges_nodup\n      rw [← (c.rotate _ ha).cons_tail_eq hr.not_nil, edges_cons] at hnd\n      simpa using (List.nodup_cons.1 hnd).1\n  -- the edge u — v is the image of a dart of c\n  obtain ⟨d, hd, hde⟩ := List.mem_map.1 he\n  rcases dart_edge_eq_mk'_iff'.1 hde with ⟨hfst, hsnd⟩ | ⟨hfst, hsnd⟩\n  · -- the dart points from u to v: cut there\n    exact key d hd hfst hsnd\n  · -- the dart points from v to u: cut there and reverse the resulting path\n    obtain ⟨p, h1, h2, h3, h4⟩ := key d hd hfst hsnd\n    exact ⟨p.reverse, h1.reverse, by simpa using h2, by simpa using h3,\n      by simpa [Sym2.eq_swap] using h4⟩\n\n\nIf the cycle is Hamiltonian then the path visits every vertex, so it is a\nHamiltonian path (IsPath.isHamiltonian_iff).\n\nlemma Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle.exists_isHamiltonian_walk_of_mem_edges [DecidableEq V]\n    {x u v : V} {c : G.Walk x x} (hc : c.IsHamiltonianCycle)\n    (he : s(u, v) ∈ c.edges) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.IsHamiltonian ∧ s(u, v) ∉ p.edges := by\n  obtain ⟨p, hp, -, hsupp, hpe⟩ := hc.isCycle.exists_isPath_of_mem_edges he\n  exact ⟨p, hp.isHamiltonian_iff.2 fun w => (hsupp w).2 (hc.mem_support w), hpe⟩\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","header":"8.8.4. The Bondy–Chvátal Theorem","id":"/Walks/#section-the-bondy-chv-tal-theorem"}});